{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a53ae9fa-194b-496d-9c28-60e54a6a0428","step":{"id":"s1","script":"In uniform circular motion, speed stays constant while the object follows a circle of radius r.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Uniform circular motion"},{"op":"figure","id":"motion","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3.8,4.2],"y":[-3.8,4.2],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"path","center":[0,0],"r":3,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"body","at":[3,0],"label":"m"},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"body"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"r"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"v=\\text{constant}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a53ae9fa-194b-496d-9c28-60e54a6a0428","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Velocity is tangent to the circle. Even at constant speed, its direction changes, so the object accelerates.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"velocity","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"vel","from":{"at":"body"},"to":[3,2.3],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"body"},"from":[3,2.3],"to":{"at":"center"},"r":0.12},{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":3,"start":20,"end":65,"arrow":true}]},{"op":"text","id":"direction","anchor":{"el":"speed","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Constant speed, changing velocity"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a53ae9fa-194b-496d-9c28-60e54a6a0428","step":{"id":"s3","script":"This acceleration points inward, toward the center. Its magnitude is speed squared divided by radius.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"acceleration","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"inward","from":{"at":"body"},"to":[1,0],"arrow":true},{"kind":"text","at":[1.9,-0.45],"text":"inward"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"accel_eq","anchor":{"el":"direction","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a_c=\\frac{v^2}{r}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At the same radius, what happens to centripetal acceleration if speed doubles?","expected_gist":"It quadruples because centripetal acceleration is proportional to speed squared."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a53ae9fa-194b-496d-9c28-60e54a6a0428","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Newton’s second law requires an inward net force. Centripetal force names that net force, supplied by forces such as tension, friction, or gravity.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"force_note","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.7,0.5],"text":"net force"},{"kind":"text","at":[0,-3.5],"text":"Toward O, not forward"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"force_eq","anchor":{"el":"accel_eq","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"F_{\\text{net,inward}}=ma_c=\\frac{mv^2}{r}"},{"op":"text","id":"meaning","anchor":{"el":"force_eq","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Not an extra force: the inward net force"}],"checkpoint":null}}
